# tools/ci-tiers.map — which CI suites a changed path can affect (px37, px40, tl29).
#
# Read by tools/ci-plan, proven by tests/ci-plan. One rule a line:
#
#   GLOB  TARGET...
#
# GLOB is a repository path matched with fnmatch (`*` crosses `/`).
# A TARGET is a suite (a job of .github/workflows/ci.yml), a group
# (`@name`, defined by a `@name = suite...` line), or one of:
#   FULL  the all tier: every suite, every leg (native and release,
#         BIOS and UEFI), whatever else changed, the `full-matrix` label
#         included (the name is px37's, when that tier was called full;
#         tl29 gave `full` to the merge gate, every suite on native BIOS);
#   ALL   every suite, on the pull-request leg;
#   NONE  nothing runs for this path (prose that no test reads).
# Every rule a path matches applies (the union), FULL wins. Beside these
# rules tools/ci-plan derives, from the tests' own text, the suites that
# read a path: tests/S and tests/S.d/ are S's; a kernel/kmain*.lu is the
# suite's whose script names it; a tools/, user/, boot/ or font/ path is
# the suite's whose script, or a tool that script names, names it
# (README in tools/ci-plan). A path no rule and no derivation matches is
# FULL: an unknown path never shrinks a run.
#
# Why a kernel module is mapped by what RUNS it, not by what imports it:
# every kernel imports every module (kernel/interrupts reaches the
# system-call table, which reaches the rest), so the import closure of
# each kmain is the whole kernel (43 to 47 of 47 modules, px37's census).
# A change that breaks the BUILD shows up in any suite, because each one
# compiles the whole package; a change that breaks BEHAVIOUR shows up in
# the suites that run the code, and those are the targets below. The
# merge gate (`tier full`, the label) runs all of them on native BIOS,
# and every trunk push and nightly (`tier all`) on every leg (tl29).

@user    = mpx3-user mpx3-loader mpx3-boreutils mpx3-console mpx3-screen mpx3-shell mpx3-threads mpx3-tree mpx3-maps mpx3-speaker mpx3-sound mpx3-orchestra mpx4-net mpx4-udp mpx4-tcp mpx4-ready mpx4-session mpx4-unix mpx4-lobo mpx5-users mpx5-accounts mpx5-jobs mpx5-yew mpx5-screen mpx5-wolf mpx6-ext2 mpx5-scent mpx5-bore mpx6-root mpx6-journal mpx6-disktools mpx6-install mpx6-fsck mpx6-games
@net     = mpx4-net mpx4-udp mpx4-tcp mpx4-ready mpx4-session mpx4-unix mpx4-lobo mpx5-jobs
@music   = mpx3-speaker mpx3-sound mpx3-orchestra mpx4-session
@console = mpx3-console mpx3-screen mpx3-shell mpx3-threads mpx3-tree mpx3-speaker mpx3-sound mpx3-orchestra mpx4-unix mpx4-session mpx5-accounts mpx5-jobs tour mpx5-yew mpx5-screen mpx5-wolf mpx6-install mpx6-games
# px65: mpx5-bore is in @user (boreutils' new utilities ask for FIFOs,
# links, sizes, syncs, random bytes, the tree's names and the console as
# a terminal) and @asuser (its user sessions run through `as`).
# px64: mpx5-scent is in @user: its image is the one whose archive holds
# a symbolic link (/bin/grep -> scent, tools/mkinitramfs' `->`), which
# kernel/tree adopts and kernel/files and kernel/process follow (exec
# through the link, a `#!` line's interpreter), and its user session runs
# through `as` (@asuser).
# px62: mpx3-maps (mappings, FIFOs, flock) is in @user (it runs programs
# over kernel/vm, kernel/tree and kernel/files) and @asuser (its user
# runs go through `as`); its probes and their builder (tools/mkmaps,
# user/elf/maps.c, fifos.c) are its own by the derivation. Its four
# boots fit the PR leg (about ten minutes with the build), so it runs in
# `tier pr` whenever a module it exercises changes.
# px47 (P5 U7): the suites whose user runs become a user through `as`
# (px39; tools/mkas builds it alone) and through `su` (px46); px48's
# job-control suite runs its probe and its shell through `as` too,
# px54's lope suite its user session, and px58's yew suite its user probe
# and yew's session. px58: mpx5-yew is in @user (yew asks for the files,
# the tree, the processes, the signals and the console a user's program
# reaches) and @console (its screen rows). px59: mpx5-screen too (its
# session is a user's; the console and the screen are what it holds).
# gm01: mpx6-games (the wolf games typed at the screen as `pax`) is in
# @user, @console and @asuser, as mpx5-yew.
@asuser  = mpx3-tree mpx3-maps mpx3-shell mpx3-boreutils mpx4-unix mpx4-tcp mpx4-udp mpx5-users mpx5-jobs mpx5-lope mpx5-aspen mpx5-lupin mpx5-yew mpx5-screen mpx5-wolf mpx5-scent mpx5-bore mpx6-games
@suuser  = mpx3-tree mpx3-shell mpx3-speaker mpx4-lobo mpx5-accounts
# px63: the disk's two suites (the block device, the file system on it);
# px66: and its partition table's (the windows, the names, a disk that boots);
# px67: and `/` on it (the root record, the tree installed, the mounts
# over it, mount(2), the end that leaves it clean). mpx6-root is in
# @user too: every program it runs is exec'd from the disk through
# kernel/tree's second store, kernel/files and kernel/process.
# px68: and its journal (the commits' order, the replay, the crash row
# gated); in @user too, for the same reason as mpx6-root.
# px79: and its nodes (/dev's block devices, raw I/O, the ioctls, the
# rescan, BLKPG, /sys's block files, several disks, mount by node).
# px69: and the disk tools (a whole disk written on PAX's vdb through
# px79's nodes, booted alone; in @user too, as mpx6-root).
@disk    = mpx6-blk mpx6-ext2 mpx6-part mpx6-root mpx6-journal mpx6-nodes mpx6-disktools mpx6-install mpx6-fsck

